Answer: ```java
continue skips the remaining statements of the current iteration and transfers control to the increment section of the for loop.
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
if (i == 2) {
continue;
}
System.out.println(i);
}
```

Output: 1 3 The iteration where i == 2 skips the remaining statement.

Answer: Control goes to the increment/decrement section of the for loop. Example:

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
if (i == 2) {
continue;
}
}
```

When continue executes, control goes to:

```java
i++;
```

and then the condition is checked again.

Answer: Unreachable code is code that can never be executed because control can never reach it. Example:

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
break;
System.out.println("Hello");
}
```

The statement:

```java
System.out.println("Hello");
```

can never execute because break transfers control out of the loop before that statement is reached. This results in an unreachable code compilation error.

